<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Muhammad Iqbal</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1877">1877</a>–<a href="/years/1938">1938</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Allamah, Hakim ul-Ummat, Mufakkir-e-Pakistan</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sir Muhammad Iqbal was an Islamic philosopher and poet of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-india">British India</a>, among the most influential Muslim thinkers of the 20th century. Born in Sialkot, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a>, he studied at Trinity College, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambridge">Cambridge</a>, qualified as a barrister at Lincoln's Inn and earned a PhD in philosophy in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/munich">Munich</a> in 1908, and he wrote in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/urdu">Urdu</a> and Persian such works as Asrar-e-Khudi and Bang-e-Dara. In his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prayagraj">Allahabad</a> Address of 1930 to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/all-india-muslim-league">All-India Muslim League</a> he set out a political framework for the Muslim-majority regions of northwestern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a>, established in 1947, nine years after his death, honours him as its national poet.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/muhammad-iqbal.jpg" alt="Muhammad Iqbal" width="330" height="473" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Iqbal Academy Pakistan · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Allama_Iqbal.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In culture the national poet <b>Muhammad Iqbal</b> shaped Urdu letters, Abdus Salam won the Nobel Prize in Physics and Malala Yousafzai became the youngest Nobel laureate; Pakistan launched the first satellite of any Muslim nation in 1990 and has won every major ICC cricket tournament.</blockquote><a href="/empire/islamic-republic-of-pakistan">Islamic Republic of Pakistan · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1930 the poet <b>Muhammad Iqbal</b> proposed a consolidated Muslim state in north-western India, and in the Lahore Resolution of March 1940 the League under Muhammad Ali Jinnah demanded “independent states” for the Muslim-majority areas, holding that Hindus and Muslims were two nations.</blockquote><a href="/movement/indian-independence-movement">Indian independence movement · Aims</a></li><li><blockquote>In British India the Khilafat movement of 1919–24 expressed Muslims’ hopes for the caliphate, and <b>Muhammad Iqbal</b>, who in 1930 outlined a Muslim state in north-western India, influenced Maududi.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/islamism">Islamism · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>&quot;Sare Jahan se Accha&quot;, formally &quot;Tarānah-e-Hindi&quot; (&quot;Anthem of the People of Hindustan&quot;), is an Urdu patriotic song for children written by the poet <b>Muhammad Iqbal</b> in the ghazal style of Urdu poetry.</blockquote><a href="/songs/sare-jahan-se-accha">Sare Jahan se Accha</a></li></ul></section>
